  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d2139f5f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To choose the number of epochs for the later experiments, we first trained the base MLP and CNN (AdamW, learning rate 0.001, batch size 64) for 30 epochs and monitored the validation loss. More epochs increase computation time and, once the validation loss starts rising, lead to overfitting. The validation loss of the MLP reached its minimum at around epoch 15, and that of the CNN at around epoch 10, after which it increased while the training loss kept falling. We use 15 epochs for both models, so that the comparison between them is fair and the slower MLP is not cut off. Because the weights of the epoch with the lowest validation loss are kept, the extra epochs cost only computation time, not accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "The number of epochs needed also depends on the optimizer and the learning rate: a small learning rate (for example SGD with 0.001) is still improving after 30 epochs with the loss bottoming out between 50 to 60 epochs. A fixed budget of 15 epochs thus penalizes slowly converging configurations. We take this into account when interpreting the results."
   ]
  },
