   "source": [
    "Here we start our experiments by comparing combinations of optimizers and learning rates for both MLP and CNN. Through some initial exploration and reading, the most important choices in neural network designs are commonly regarded as the choices of optimizer and learning rates as well as the architecture choices (i.e. deeper or wider neural networks). For architecture, greater depth/width increases the capacity to learn which may improve the fit but could also lead to overfitting. Whether it would be best to first explore architecture or the learning rates/optimizers is a bit difficult to establish. A bad learning rate could make good architecture look terrible and a poor architecture could make a well tuned learning rate look terrible. By looking at a grid of optimizer and learning rate combinations first with a base architecture however we hope to at least gain some insight into roughly what values of learning rates are ideal for the different optimizers and how well they actually perform. We see that AdamW and Adam with weight decay set to 0 amount to identical results, consistent with what we know about the optimizers (they are the same algorithm when weight_decay is set to 0). Furthermore with a learning rate of 0.1 Adam/AdamW in CNN collapse to what amounts to be random guessing (10 classes and 10% validation accuracy), and in MLP they too perform significantly worse over any other combination. However for MLP they do not seem to collapse to random guessing, instead reaching a validation accuracy of 52.65%\n",
    "\n",
    "We can see that the CNN outperforms the MLP in nearly all combinations by roughly 1-3 percentage points in validation accuracy. The only exceptions are Adam/AdamW with learning rates of 0.1 where CNN collapses to 10% validation accuracy whilst MLP stays around 53%. The best learning rate for AdamW is 0.001 for both models, whereas SGD needs a higher rate in the range of 0.01-0.03. This is because Adam/AdamW divide the gradient by an estimate of its current size so each weight moves the size of the lr per step whereas with SGD the step is scaled with the gradients which are usually small. You can see the effect this has on SGD with the lowest learning rate of 0.001 by the fact that it is the worst performing learning rate for SGD in both MLP and CNN models.\n",
    "\n",
    "The differences between the top configurations are small. For the CNN, the best AdamW and SGD configurations reach 91.85% and 91.23% validation accuracy, and for the MLP the best two are less than one percentage point apart. With a single seed, gaps of this size could simply reflect the random weight initialization and the random order of the batches, so any conclusion about which configuration is best would hinge on one run. We therefore repeat the grid with two additional seeds (68 and 69) and compare the means and standard deviations over the three seeds, to see which configurations perform well consistently for this initial optimizer/learning rate configuration."
   ]
  },
