  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1aebc2b3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The above table shows the activation, initialization and regularization sweeps for the MLP (base: AdamW with a learning rate of 0.001, two hidden layers of 128 nodes, ReLU, default initialization, no dropout and no weight decay). The activation functions span only 0.28 points (89.02-89.30%), which is within the run-to-run noise established in Stage 1 when comparing different seeds, so we do not rank them. Kaiming initialization is 0.51 points above the default (89.53% versus 89.02%) and 0.43 above the next best (Xavier, 89.10%), which is just below our noise threshold of 0.6 and of interest but not conclusive. Dropout of 0.1 is indistinguishable from no dropout (89.08% versus 89.02%, loss 0.3101 versus 0.3109), whereas higher values lower the accuracy: 0.3 by 0.34 points, which is within the noise, and 0.5 by 0.99 points (loss 0.3408), which is above the threshold. Weight decay of 0.001 is slightly above no weight decay (89.38% versus 89.02%), but larger values lower the accuracy again (88.88% and 88.65%), all within the noise, so we see no clear benefit in weight decay.\n",
    "\n",
    "A possible explanation for the negative effect of dropout is that this MLP has little redundancy: its first layer already has to compress 784 pixels into 128 nodes (a width of 32 was too small in Stage 2), so removing units loses information the network needs. In addition, regularization slows convergence, so the 15-epoch budget may be too short for the regularized models."
   ]
  },
